AB  - Concentrations of air pollutants are increasing along with societal development and have the potential to be a serious human health risk (e.g. heart and lung disease). Thus, it is necessary to predict and evaluate air pollutant concentrations and take certain actions to reduce their impacts on individuals. This work describes the application of three methods - Long short term memory (LSTM), Light gradient boost machine (Light GBM) and Random Forests - in an air pollutant concentration prediction task. Specifically, the three approaches were used to predict the concentrations of PM2.5, PM10, NO2 and O-3 in Nanjing and Changzhou City, China. The results showed that Random Forests performed the best among the algorithms in predicting PM2.5 and PM10. Then, applying Random Forests to predict the concentrations of all four air pollutants in both cities, we found that its prediction of PM2.5 was the most accurate, possibly because the PM10, O-3 and NO2 data fluctuated obviously during the study period.
